Audience: EWG, LEWG
Editor: YexuanXiao <bizwen@nykz.org>
!Source: [cancellation](https://github.com/YexuanXiao/cancellation)
Markup Shorthands: markdown on
Abstract: Allow a suspended coroutine to be cancelled. Cancellation is a
          lightweight marking operation performed while the coroutine is
          suspended; the effect takes place when the coroutine is resumed.
          This provides a third, exception-free exit path for coroutines,
          enabling efficient propagation of cancellation across nested
          coroutine hierarchies without throwing and catching at every
          coroutine frame.
</pre>

# Motivation # {#motivation}

C++20 coroutines are extremely flexible in most respects, with one notable
exception: the ways in which a coroutine can *exit* are limited. A
coroutine can only exit in two ways: by returning a value, and by throwing
an exception. This proposal argues that a third exit path,
*collaborative cancellation*, is missing. The absence of such a mechanism
forces coroutines to rely on exceptions for exit, with the exception
rethrown at every coroutine frame along the way.

The key insight is that the defining feature of a coroutine is that it can
be *suspended*. A suspended coroutine can be *destroyed*, and a suspended
coroutine can be *resumed*. This proposal observes that a suspended
coroutine can also be *cancelled*.

We like suspended coroutines. A suspended coroutine already contains
all of the information needed to destroy it; it therefore also contains
all of the information needed to cancel it.

## The two exit paths ## {#two-exit-paths}

1. *Returning a value.* If a coroutine is cancelled by returning a value
    (for example, by checking `std::stop_token::stop_requested()` and
    returning early), the coroutine's return type typically has to be
    changed to something like `std::expected`, and every
    `await_resume` has to return `std::expected` as well.
    This has not been widely adopted.

2. *Throwing an exception.* Using exceptions to cancel coroutines is the
    popular approach, and most libraries do it. But this approach has a
    cost: exceptions are expensive. When a child coroutine is cancelled,
    the exception is caught by the child's
    `promise::unhandled_exception()`, stored, and rethrown into the parent
    coroutine. This happens at *every* coroutine frame along the way.

Consider a task hierarchy of depth N. Cancelling the innermost task
causes O(N) throw/catch pairs, each of which involves unwinding a
coroutine frame, storing an `exception_ptr`, and rethrowing it in the
parent. For deep hierarchies this cost dominates.

I think we should all agree that exceptions resulting from most `co_await`
and `co_yield` expressions do not need to be caught by user-written
try-catch blocks, and should simply be propagated to their caller, as is
customary in C++. However, the current coroutine mechanism does not provide
a facility to directly convey either "cancellation" or "exception" state.

## Cancellation as a third exit path ## {#third-exit-path}

A coroutine can be suspended, and a suspended coroutine can be resumed or
destroyed. This proposal adds a third operation on suspended coroutines:
*cancellation*.

Destruction of a suspended coroutine can be decomposed into two steps:

1. destroying all live local variables of the coroutine body (objects whose
    lifetimes are dynamic), and
2. destroying the coroutine's common state (parameters, coroutine-internal
    state, and the promise object, which live for the whole coroutine).

Cancelling a coroutine is *not* destroying it; cancellation does strictly
less than destruction. There is only one thing it can choose to do: destroy
the coroutine body's local variables, same as step 1. Compilers already
generate a separate set of code to destroy the local variables; this
independence, however, is currently not exploited: there is no mechanism
that puts it to use on its own, without also destroying the common state.

How does cancellation fit into the existing coroutine design? The key is to
*mimic exceptions*. Today, an exception is thrown during a resume; an
uncaught exception makes the coroutine enter `unhandled_exception()`. With
this proposal, cancellation takes effect immediately when the coroutine is
resumed; an unhandled cancellation makes the coroutine enter
`unhandled_cancellation()`. The two concepts are analogous. Unlike
exceptions, however, cancellation as proposed here leverages the
coroutine's own machinery, and is therefore more efficient.

This leads to an apparent contradiction: a coroutine should be cancellable
only while suspended, but cancellation should take effect during execution.

The resolution is the key design idea of this proposal: **while suspended,
cancelling a coroutine is marking it; when the coroutine is resumed, the
mark takes effect.**

# Design considerations # {#design-considerations}

The mechanism proposed here is deliberately minimal and, we believe,
self-consistent:

- while a coroutine is suspended, `request_cancel()` marks it as
    *cancelled* (a cheap, non-throwing, non-destroying operation);
- when a cancelled coroutine is subsequently *resumed*, the mark takes
    effect: instead of continuing at the suspend point, the coroutine invokes
    `promise.unhandled_cancellation()` and then
    `goto final-suspend;`, mirroring what happens when an exception escapes
    the coroutine body.

`request_cancel()` has no effect other than setting a flag in the coroutine
state. Cancellation is a one-shot operation: the cancellation state can be
consumed only once, and calling `request_cancel()` again is undefined
behavior. The coroutine is suspended at the time of the call, so there is
no concurrency involved.

`cancel_requested()` exists so that the promise does not need to store a
separate `bool` for observers to check. The cancellation state is part of
the coroutine frame and remains queryable while the frame is alive.

## Cancellation is opt-in ## {#handler-optional}

`unhandled_cancellation()` is not required to be defined by every promise
type; cancellation is *opt-in* at the level of code generation. A coroutine
whose promise type does not declare `unhandled_cancellation()` is not
*cancellation-aware*: the compiler generates no cancellation code for it
(no flag in the frame, no checks at the resumption points), it carries no
runtime cost, and its behavior is unchanged.

## Exceptions escaping the cancellation handler ## {#cwg2934}

An exception escaping `unhandled_cancellation()` behaves exactly like one
escaping `unhandled_exception()`: the coroutine state is destroyed and the
exception propagates to the caller or resumer ([[#wording-core]]).

The current wording for `unhandled_exception()` says the coroutine "is
considered suspended at the final suspend point" when the exception
escapes; [[CWG2934]] confirms this is a wording
defect and implementations destroy the coroutine state instead. This
proposal specifies the corrected semantics for `unhandled_cancellation()`.

## Cancellation and exceptions are interconvertible ## {#interconvertible}

Cancellation can be converted to an exception where an exception is
expected, and vice versa. This is possible because, when the awaiter knows
the promise type, it can exchange information through the promise after
cancelling the coroutine, or through `unhandled_cancellation()`:

- an exception produced by a child coroutine can be converted into a
    cancellation of the parent, so that intermediate coroutines do not pay
    the cost of throwing and catching;
- a cancellation of a child coroutine can be converted into an exception
    in the parent, for APIs that must surface cancellation as an exception.

[[#example-no-rethrow]] shows awaiters that perform these conversions in
both directions.

# Examples # {#examples}

## Interoperating with `std::stop_token` ## {#example-stop-token}

`std::stop_token` answers *whether* cancellation has been requested;
`request_cancel()` delivers that request to a suspended coroutine, and
`unhandled_cancellation()` provides a uniform place to respond to it:

```cpp
struct stop_token_awaiter {
    std::stop_token t;
    bool cancelled = false;

    bool await_ready() { return false; }

    template <class Promise>
    void await_suspend(std::coroutine_handle<Promise> handle) {
        if (t.stop_requested()) {
            cancelled = true;
            handle.request_cancel();
        }
    }

    void await_resume() {
        if (cancelled) {
            std::unreachable();
        }
    }
};
```

## Cancelling when a task cannot be scheduled ## {#example-schedule}

```cpp
void await_suspend(std::coroutine_handle<promise> handle) {
    if (!post_to_queue(handle)) {
        handle.request_cancel();
    }
}
```

## Propagating cancellation between coroutines ## {#example-propagate}

To propagate cancellation state between coroutines, an awaiter can be
designed as follows:

```cpp
struct propagate_cancellation_awaiter {
    std::coroutine_handle<promise> child;

    bool await_ready() { return false; }

    void await_suspend(std::coroutine_handle<promise> parent) {
        if (child.cancel_requested()) {
            parent.request_cancel();
        }
        // ...
    }
    void await_resume() {}
};
```

## Avoiding repeated throwing and catching ## {#example-no-rethrow}

The new API can be used to avoid throwing and catching exceptions in every
coroutine frame. Different awaiters can convert exceptions to cancellation,
and cancellation to exceptions:

```cpp
struct exception_to_cancellation_awaiter {
    std::coroutine_handle<promise> child;
    bool await_ready() { return false; }
    void await_suspend(std::coroutine_handle<promise> parent) {
        if (auto &&e = child.promise().get_exception()) {
            parent.promise().set_exception(e);
            parent.request_cancel();
        }
        // ...
    }
    void await_resume() {}
};

struct cancellation_to_exception_awaiter {
    std::coroutine_handle<promise> child;
    std::exception_ptr curr_e;
    bool await_ready() { return false; }
    void await_suspend(std::coroutine_handle<promise> parent) {
        if (auto &&e = child.promise().get_exception(); child.cancel_requested() && !e) {
            curr_e = std::make_exception_ptr(cancelled_exception{});
        } else if (e) {
            curr_e = e;
        }
        // ...
    }
    void await_resume() {
        if (curr_e) {
            std::rethrow_exception(curr_e);
        }
    }
};
```

Intermediate coroutines can propagate the exception as described above,
avoiding the cost of throwing and catching at every frame.

## Nested generators ## {#example-generator}

Consider a nested `std::generator`. Today, when the innermost
generator throws, the exception is stored and rethrown at every nesting
level: `unhandled_exception()` of every intermediate generator captures the
exception, and the recursive awaiter rethrows it into the parent. With this
proposal, the innermost generator stores the exception once, marks the
parent as cancelled, and every intermediate generator merely propagates the
cancellation mark. Only the root generator, at the boundary with the
client, converts the mark back into an exception (a single throw/catch
pair regardless of the nesting depth). See the accompanying benchmark
([[#performance]]) for measurements.

# Impact on the standard # {#impact-on-the-standard}

This feature is introverted and independent, and has no impact on other
language features.

# Impact on existing code # {#impact-on-existing-code}

No existing code becomes ill-formed: coroutines whose promise types do not
declare `unhandled_cancellation()` are unaffected (see [[#handler-optional]]).
Because the new member functions are additive, they do not break binary
compatibility of existing coroutine frames (see [[#impact-on-abi]]).

# Impact on ABI # {#impact-on-abi}

Currently, the coroutine frames of MSVC, GCC, and Clang are similar to:

```cpp
struct coro_frame {
    using op_t = void(coro_frame*);
    op_t* resume_func;
    op_t* destroy_func;
    promise_type promise;
    // other coroutine state and data that are not part of the ABI surface
    coro_state state;
}
```

This proposal makes the coroutine frame of a cancellation-aware coroutine as:

```cpp
struct coro_frame {
    using op_t = void(coro_frame*);
    op_t* resume_func;
    op_t* destroy_func;
    bool cancelled;
    promise_type promise;
    // other coroutine state and data that are not part of the ABI surface
    coro_state state;
}
```

This requires that `coroutine_handle::promise` and
`coroutine_handle::from_promise` must correctly distinguish whether the
coroutine pointed to by `*this` is not cancellation-aware or cancellation-aware
(by detecting the existence of `promise::unhandled_cancellation`).

A base-class promise can obtained from a coroutine whose promise type a
derived class of only when the handle's `Promise` type and the coroutine's
actual promise type have the same cancellation-awareness (either both
cancellation-aware or both not). For example, if `B` is a base class of a
cancellation-aware promise type `D`, then `coroutine_handle<B>::promise()`
may only be called if `B` also declares `unhandled_cancellation()`.

If the committee agrees to update the ABI rather than extending the existing
ABI, so that the coroutine frame always has the `cancelled` field, then
`request_cancel` and `cancel_requested` on non-cancellation-aware coroutines
can have deterministic behavior while also simplifying the standard library.

Whether the existing ABI is extended or updated, the existing `done`, `resume`,
and `destroy` remain unaffected.

# Performance # {#performance}

To validate the motivation, we compared exception propagation through nested
generators with and without the cancellation chain, using the stock
`std::generator` implementation (one throw/catch pair per nesting level) and
a variant that uses `request_cancel()` / `unhandled_cancellation()` to
propagate the failure. The innermost generator throws after yielding a few
elements; the benchmark measures one round per failing iteration, plus a
non-throwing baseline. A baseline with a single non-nested generator
(`depth 0`, throwing and non-throwing) is included for reference: it shows
the cost of one generator whose exception escapes directly, without any
intermediate frames.

Since libc++ has not yet implemented `std::generator`, the `std::generator`
used in this benchmark is based on the ongoing
[LLVM#201854](https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/pull/201854).

The environment is Clang trunk (20260817), -O2, x86-64.

<table class="data">
  <caption>Benchmark results (ns/round)</caption>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>nesting depth <th>impl <th>normal (ns/round) <th>failing (ns/round) <th>overhead (ns/round) <th>speedup
  <tbody>
    <tr><td rowspan=2>3 <td>stock <td>784 <td>21,448 <td>20,664 <td rowspan=2>2.9×
    <tr><td>cancellation <td>845 <td>8,165 <td>7,320
    <tr><td rowspan=2>5 <td>stock <td>1,186 <td>29,729 <td>28,543 <td rowspan=2>4.1×
    <tr><td>cancellation <td>1,275 <td>7,635 <td>6,360
    <tr><td>0 (single generator) <td>— <td>182 <td>7,619 <td>7,437 <td>—
</table>

The cancellation chain keeps the failing path essentially constant as the
nesting depth grows (8,165 ns at depth 3 vs 7,635 ns at depth 5) and is
already close to the single-generator baseline (7,619 ns): adding nesting
levels costs only a few hundred nanoseconds of flag-set/check and
symmetric-transfer per level. The stock implementation grows linearly
(21,448 ns at depth 3 vs 29,729 ns at depth 5), i.e. one full throw/catch
pair per nesting level on top of the single-generator cost. The normal path
is within noise of the stock implementation for both depths (a single flag
load and branch per resume), and both are an order of magnitude below the
failing path.

# Proposed wording # {#proposed-wording}

[Editor's note: The following wording is relative to [[!N5055]].]

## Core language ## {#wording-core}

Change the value of `__cpp_impl_coroutine` in [cpp.predefined] to `2026????L`.

Insert a new paragraph after [dcl.fct.def.coroutine] paragraph 4:

> A coroutine whose promise type declares a member function
> unhandled_cancellation is cancellation-aware. If a cancellation-aware
> coroutine is resumed and its cancellation state is set, and the coroutine
> is not suspended at final suspend point, the resumption does not
> continue at the suspend point; instead, the coroutine behaves as if the
> following were executed:
>
> ```
> promise.unhandled_cancellation() ;
> goto final-suspend ;
> ```
>
> [Note: The cancellation state is set by
> `request_cancel()` ([coroutine.handle.cancel]). — end note]

Insert a new paragraph after [dcl.fct.def.coroutine] paragraph 18:

> If the evaluation of the expression `promise.unhandled_cancellation()`
> exits via an exception, the coroutine state is destroyed and the
> exception propagates to the caller or resumer.

## Library ## {#wording-library}

Change the value of `__cpp_lib_coroutine` in [version.syn] to `2026????L`.

Modify [coroutine.handle.general] as follows:

Add to `coroutine_handle<void>` and to `coroutine_handle<Promise>`:

> // [coroutine.handle.cancel], cancellation
>
> void request_cancel() const;
>
> bool cancel_requested() const;

Modify [coroutine.handle.promise] and [coroutine.handle.con], add a
precondition to `promise()` and `from_promise()`:

> The promise type of the coroutine and the `Promise` template argument of
> the handle have the same cancellation-awareness.

Insert a new subclause [coroutine.handle.cancel], "Cancellation" after
[coroutine.handle.promise]:

> bool cancel_requested() const;
>
> *Preconditions:* `*this` refers to a suspended coroutine. The coroutine has
> not already been cancelled. The coroutine is cancellation-aware.
>
> *Effects:* Sets the cancellation state of the coroutine.
>
> void request_cancel() const;
>
> *Preconditions:* `*this` refers to a suspended coroutine. The coroutine is
> cancellation-aware.
>
> *Returns:* `true` if the cancellation state of the coroutine is set, otherwise
> `false`.

